+### Depth Supervision
+A training technique to improve reconstructed geometry by providing depth priors using off-the-shelf depth estimation models, resulting in better geometry and reduced number of floaters [S7, unofficial]. It requires refitting all depth maps against a prior point cloud to the same scale; maps that fail refitting are discarded [S7, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The integration of geometric supervision into NeRF optimization emerged as a response to the "shape-radiance ambiguity," where sparse or textureless inputs lead to degenerate solutions such as floating artifacts or incorrect geometry [S1, 8687-depth-supervised-nerf.md]. Early work, such as DS-NeRF, leveraged the sparse 3D keypoints naturally produced by Structure-from-Motion (SfM) pipelines to regularize the density field via KL divergence between rendered and prior depth distributions [S1, 8687-depth-supervised-nerf.md]. Subsequent methods expanded this to include dense monocular depth priors, stereo depth refinement, and structural consistency constraints to handle challenging indoor and outdoor scenes [S1, 9371-depth-structural-consistency-regularization.md, 10185-statistical-depth-refinement.md]. The family also includes techniques that use depth to guide sampling strategies [7496-guided-optimization-of-nerf.md] or to enforce consistency between hierarchical coarse and fine models [S1].
+The problem of shape-radiance ambiguity in NeRF optimization was identified early on, particularly in sparse-view settings where the model could overfit to training images or produce "floaters" in empty space [S1, S2]. Depth-Supervised NeRF (DS-NeRF) was one of the first methods to introduce explicit geometric constraints using sparse 3D keypoints from SfM, using a KL divergence loss to align rendered depth distributions with surface priors [S1, S4]. Subsequent work explored various forms of supervision: dense monocular depth priors [S2, S4], architectural priors for indoor scenes [S3], and consistency losses between coarse and fine hierarchical models [S1]. The field has expanded to include generalized ray supervision for light transport [S14127] and 3D point-based registration [S12693], reflecting a broader trend toward integrating explicit geometric signals into implicit neural representations.
 
